--TEST--
Core\Storage refuses a key that is not one segment rather than resolving it, so no key reaches past the disk's root — ADR 0082 § 2, ADR 0095
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The rule `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/storage.rs`'s module doc argues: a key names
// an object and never a path through the disk. Every spelling that could have
// made it something else — a `..`, a separator either way round, an absolute
// path, a leading dot, an empty name — is refused rather than stripped, which
// is ADR 0095's direction and is what keeps the `fs.*` grant over `root`
// exactly the set of objects.
//
// The refusal happens before any door is asked, so this case needs neither a
// grant nor a disk that exists.
try {
    Core\Storage::put("uploads", "../escape", "x" as bytes);
} catch (RuntimeError $error) {
    echo $error->message, "\n";
}

try {
    Core\Storage::get("uploads", "nested/key");
} catch (RuntimeError $error) {
    echo $error->message, "\n";
}

try {
    Core\Storage::delete("uploads", "");
} catch (RuntimeError $error) {
    echo $error->message, "\n";
}

// The same refusal for the shapes that look least like a path: a Windows
// separator, and a name the operating system would read as the directory
// itself. One sentence covers all of them because they are one question.
array<string> $spellings = ["a\\b", ".", "..", ".hidden", "/absolute"];
int $refused = 0;
foreach ($spellings as string $spelling) {
    try {
        Core\Storage::get("uploads", $spelling);
        echo "accepted: ", $spelling, "\n";
    } catch (RuntimeError $error) {
        $refused = $refused + 1;
    }
}
echo "refused ", $refused, " of ", Core\Arr::count($spellings), "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
[storage.uploads]
root = "./objects"

[capabilities.fs]
read = ["./objects"]
write = ["./objects"]
--EXPECT--
Core\Storage::put: `../escape` is not an object key — one to 255 bytes of ASCII letters, digits, `.`, `-` and `_`, not beginning with a `.`. A key names an object on the disk and never a path through it, so a separator is refused rather than resolved
Core\Storage::get: `nested/key` is not an object key — one to 255 bytes of ASCII letters, digits, `.`, `-` and `_`, not beginning with a `.`. A key names an object on the disk and never a path through it, so a separator is refused rather than resolved
Core\Storage::delete: `` is not an object key — one to 255 bytes of ASCII letters, digits, `.`, `-` and `_`, not beginning with a `.`. A key names an object on the disk and never a path through it, so a separator is refused rather than resolved
refused 5 of 5
